        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Harlem Renaissance</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">Harlem in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City, above all; other urban areas of the Northeastern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midwestern-united-states">Midwestern United States</a>; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where Josephine Baker performed and Francophone Black writers from the colonies took up its influence.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Roots in the end of the Civil War in 1865 and the Great Migration; a first stage from the late 1910s; its zenith from about 1924, when Opportunity held a party for Black writers attended by white publishers, to the stock-market crash of 1929; an abrupt decline in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> of the 1930s.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alain-leroy-locke">Alain Locke</a>, its “dean”, editor of The New Negro; the writers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langston-hughes">Langston Hughes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-mckay">Claude McKay</a>, Zora Neale Hurston, Countee Cullen, Jean Toomer, Jessie Fauset, Nella Larsen and James Weldon Johnson; Hubert Harrison, “The Father of Harlem Radicalism”; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a>; Duke Ellington, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-armstrong">Louis Armstrong</a> and Bessie Smith in music; the painter Aaron Douglas and the sculptor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusta-savage">Augusta Savage</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">Wider publication of Black writers by mainstream houses, Black music and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> on Broadway and in the concert hall, a new racial pride that changed how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> were seen, and a foundation for the civil rights movement; an abrupt end in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>.</span></div>
