    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Driven by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-migration-african-american">Great Migration</a> out of the Jim Crow South and a renewed militancy in the struggle for civil rights, the movement made Harlem the centre of a flowering of African-American literature, music, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and art.</li><li>Its idea of the New Negro, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alain-leroy-locke">Alain Locke</a>’s 1925 anthology, held that through literature, art and music Black Americans could challenge racism and stereotypes and “uplift” the race.</li><li>It brought jazz to Broadway and Black writers into mainstream publishing, but it depended heavily on white patrons and publishers, and it ended abruptly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Shuffle Along, a musical of 1921 produced, composed, directed and written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a>, ran for 504 performances on Broadway; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langston-hughes">Langston Hughes</a> later wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>’s Black Renaissance “began with Shuffle Along”.</p></aside>
    </section>

